DSP.mod(14,{"m14-strong-project":{"id":"m14-strong-project","slug":"what-a-strong-project-demonstrates","m":14,"title":"What a strong project demonstrates","min":12,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":[],"tags":["projects","portfolio"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"What interviewers look for in a data science project and how to tell whether yours demonstrates it, with a self-assessment rubric.","keyTerms":["problem framing","baseline","leakage","metrics","explainability","deployment","README","reflection"],"interviewQs":["Why should I hire you based on this project?","What is the difference between a tutorial project and a strong one?"],"mistakes":["Copying tutorials without original thinking","Reporting 99% accuracy without questioning it","Notebook dumps with no narrative"],"practice":"Score one of your projects from 0 to 2 on each of the nine items and pick the two weakest to fix.","related":["m14-portfolio","m14-deep-dive"],"points":"1. Problem framing: a real question with a clear user/decision.\n2. Data work: messy real data (scraped, API, public datasets), cleaning, joins, quality checks.\n3. EDA with insights (not just plots).\n4. Sound modeling: baseline first, proper validation, no leakage, tuning, error analysis.\n5. Right metrics aligned to business cost.\n6. Explainability: feature importance/SHAP, sanity checks.\n7. Delivery: a dashboard, API, or app, plus a recommendation.\n8. Engineering hygiene: GitHub repo, README, requirements, reproducible code.\n9. Reflection: limitations, what you would do next."},"intuitive":"<p>A strong project is a small, complete story: a real question, messy real data, a fair test of ideas, an honest result, and a way for someone to use it. Interviewers spend a large part of a technical round on your projects because they are the one place where you are the expert and they can probe how you think. A few deep projects that you can explain line by line beat many shallow ones.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> &quot;Walk me through your best project&quot; decides a lot of interviews.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Two or three projects, each a complete story, each one you can defend end to end.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A strong project shows the whole path, not just a model. It starts from a real problem and a clear question, uses real, messy data, and describes the cleaning and the choices. It sets a baseline and compares models fairly with proper validation. It chooses a metric tied to the problem and interprets errors. It ends with a result that someone could act on, plus limitations and next steps. Even better, it is deployed or at least reproducible, with a readable repository. Interviewers use projects to judge depth, so I should be able to explain every decision and what I would change. The catch: a Kaggle-style accuracy chase on a clean dataset says less than a modest, well-reasoned end-to-end project.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Upgrade a tutorial project into an interview project","html":"<p><strong>Starting point:</strong> &quot;Titanic survival prediction with a random forest, 84% accuracy.&quot;</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ask a real question that someone owns.</strong> Swap the dataset for a decision: &quot;Which customers of a subscription business are likely to cancel next month, so that retention can call the top 10%?&quot; Public telecom or e-commerce churn data works.</li>\n<li><strong>Make the data honest:</strong> document the source, the grain, missing values and leakage risks, and compute features as of a prediction date.</li>\n<li><strong>Set a baseline:</strong> &quot;customers with three or more support tickets&quot; as a simple rule, then logistic regression. Report how much the final model beats each.</li>\n<li><strong>Validate the way it would be used:</strong> train on earlier months, test on a later month.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose a business metric:</strong> precision among the top 10% (call capacity), with an estimated money value per correct call.</li>\n<li><strong>Do an error analysis:</strong> where does the model fail? For example, customers in their first month with no history.</li>\n<li><strong>Deliver something usable:</strong> a small app or notebook that takes a customer file and returns the call list, plus a one-page summary with limitations.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The algorithm hardly changed. The project became strong because it now has a decision, honest validation, a baseline, a business metric and something usable at the end.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-nine-signals-and-a-self-scoring-rubric","text":"The nine signals, and a self-scoring rubric","level":3},{"id":"h-tutorial-copy-vs-strong-project","text":"Tutorial copy vs strong project","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Problem framing:</strong> a real question with a clear user/decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Data work:</strong> messy real data (scraped, API, public datasets), cleaning, joins, quality checks.</li>\n<li><strong>EDA with insights</strong> (not just plots).</li>\n<li><strong>Sound modeling:</strong> baseline first, proper validation, no leakage, tuning, error analysis.</li>\n<li><strong>Right metrics</strong> aligned to business cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Explainability:</strong> feature importance/SHAP, sanity checks.</li>\n<li><strong>Delivery:</strong> a dashboard, API, or app, plus a recommendation.</li>\n<li><strong>Engineering hygiene:</strong> GitHub repo, README, requirements, reproducible code.</li>\n<li><strong>Reflection:</strong> limitations, what you would do next.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-nine-signals-and-a-self-scoring-rubric\">The nine signals, and a self-scoring rubric</h3>\n<p>Score each from 0 (missing) to 2 (strong):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Signal</th><th>What a 2 looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Problem framing</strong></td><td>a real question, a named user, a decision that changes</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Data work</strong></td><td>messy real data (scraped, API, public), joins, cleaning, quality checks</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>EDA with insight</strong></td><td>findings that changed what you did, not just plots</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Sound modelling</strong></td><td>baseline first, correct validation (no leakage), tuning, error analysis</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Right metrics</strong></td><td>aligned to business cost, with a stated threshold</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Explainability</strong></td><td>feature importance or SHAP and sanity checks</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><strong>Delivery</strong></td><td>a dashboard, API or app plus a recommendation</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><strong>Engineering hygiene</strong></td><td>GitHub repo, README, requirements, reproducible code</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><strong>Reflection</strong></td><td>limitations and what you would do next</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A total of 14+ out of 18 is interview-ready; under 10 means the project will crumble under &quot;why?&quot; questions. Fix the weakest two first.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-tutorial-copy-vs-strong-project\">Tutorial copy vs strong project</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tutorial copy</th><th>Strong project</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Titanic, Iris, MNIST as the headline</td><td>a real dataset tied to a real decision (churn, ETA, credit risk)</td></tr><tr><td>One model, accuracy reported</td><td>baseline vs model families, metric chosen for cost, error analysis</td></tr><tr><td>Random split, no thought about leakage</td><td>time- or group-aware split, leakage audit documented</td></tr><tr><td>Notebook with no narrative</td><td>README with problem, approach, results table, limitations, how to run</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Deployed&quot; (actually a notebook)</td><td>working API or app with a tiny demo</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout interview\"><div class=\"callout-t\">In the interview</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>If you cannot explain the math or assumptions behind the model in your own project, or cannot justify why you split the data the way you did, the project works against you. Only include what you can defend.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m14-strong-project~1","q":"You have 99% accuracy on your fraud detection project. What is the first thing an interviewer will ask and how should you answer?","a":"<p>They will ask about class balance and leakage. A good answer: &quot;Fraud was 0.6% of rows, so accuracy is meaningless (a model that always says &#39;legit&#39; scores 99.4%). I evaluated with PR-AUC and recall at a fixed precision; the baseline logistic regression got PR-AUC 0.31 and the final LightGBM 0.47. I checked leakage by making sure features used only information before the transaction time and by splitting on time.&quot; Show you already asked the tough questions yourself.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-strong-project~ca0_1","q":"What separates a strong project from a tutorial copy?","a":"<p>An original question, messy real data, justified choices, a baseline and proper validation, error analysis, and a clear actionable conclusion.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-strong-project~ca0_2","q":"Why include a baseline?","a":"<p>It shows whether the complex model adds value, and proves you evaluate rather than assume.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-strong-project~ca0_3","q":"Name two signals of reproducibility.","a":"<p>A README with setup steps and pinned dependencies, and fixed random seeds with a runnable script or notebook order.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m14-strong-project~ta1_1","q":"<p>A candidate&#39;s project reads: &quot;Sentiment analysis on movie reviews using BERT, 93% accuracy.&quot; Name three upgrades that would make it stronger in an interview.</p>\n","a":"<p>(1) Tie it to a decision: for example, route negative product reviews or support tickets to an urgent queue, measured by how quickly they get answered. (2) Add a baseline and a comparison: TF-IDF with logistic regression against BERT, with the accuracy gain weighed against the extra cost and latency. (3) Add error analysis on sarcasm, mixed reviews or Hinglish, and evaluate on data from a different source or time, not only the benchmark test split. Deploying a small demo endpoint is a fourth.</p>\n"}]},"m14-portfolio":{"id":"m14-portfolio","slug":"recommended-portfolio-and-project-ideas","m":14,"title":"Recommended portfolio and project ideas","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m14-strong-project"],"tags":["projects","portfolio"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"A recommended portfolio of three to four projects across categories, with India-relevant ideas, datasets, skills demonstrated, and how to pick and scope them.","keyTerms":["churn","credit risk","fraud","forecasting","NLP","recommender","RAG","A/B test analysis","deployment","datasets"],"interviewQs":["Which projects would you build to get a data scientist job in India?","Where do you find data?"],"mistakes":["Picking too many similar projects","Choosing a dataset with no real decision attached","Overscoping and finishing nothing"],"practice":"Choose three projects from different rows of the table, write a one-paragraph problem statement for each and pick the datasets.","related":["m14-strong-project","m14-template"],"points":"- Type - Project ideas (India-relevant) - Skills shown\n1 - Classification (business) - Customer churn (telecom/OTT/SaaS); loan default/credit risk with WoE/IV scorecard; UPI/credit-card fraud detection - Imbalance, feature engineering, metric choice, explainability\n2 - Forecasting / regression - Demand forecasting for retail/quick commerce (Kaggle M5, Rossmann); delivery-time (ETA) prediction; used-car price (Cars24/CarDekho data) - Time-aware CV, lag features, error analysis\n3 - NLP - Sentiment/aspect analysis of Zomato/Amazon/Play Store reviews; support-ticket classification; resume-JD matching; Hinglish sentiment - Text preprocessing, TF-IDF baseline vs transformer\n4 - Recommender - Movie/restaurant/product recommender with popularity, item-item CF, and matrix factorization; evaluate Precision@K/NDCG - Cold start, ranking metrics\n5 - GenAI/LLM - RAG chatbot on RBI/SEBI circulars, insurance policy PDFs or company filings; text-to-SQL assistant; report summarizer - Chunking, embeddings, vector DB, evaluation\n6 - Analytics / experimentation - Cohort/retention + funnel analysis in SQL; A/B test analysis on a public dataset (Cookie Cats, Criteo uplift); RFM segmentation; Power BI/Tableau dashboard - Business storytelling, SQL, stats\n7 - Deployment / engineering - Serve any of the above with FastAPI + Docker; Streamlit app; deploy to Render/Hugging Face Spaces/AWS/GCP free tier; add MLflow tracking - Production readiness\n8 - Big data (optional) - PySpark pipeline on large NYC taxi or e-commerce events dataset; Airflow DAG - Scale and pipelines\n\nData sources: Kaggle, UCI, Google Dataset Search, data.gov.in, RBI/NSO/NSE datasets, Open-Meteo, public APIs, Hugging Face datasets, your own scraped data (respect ToS).\n\nAvoid: Titanic/Iris/MNIST as headline projects, tutorial copies without original thinking, notebook dumps with no narrative."},"intuitive":"<p>Pick projects like you would pick dishes for a tasting menu: variety, and each one shows a different skill. One business classification problem (churn or credit risk), one forecasting or regression, one text or GenAI project, and one piece that shows you can take something to production (an API or an app). Choose a real dataset, a real decision, and scope it small enough to finish and polish.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> A portfolio with a clear spread tells the interviewer what you can do at a glance.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Three or four finished projects across categories beat ten half-done ones.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A good portfolio has two to four projects that show range. Typical set: an analytics project with SQL and a dashboard answering a business question; a predictive ML project with proper validation and error analysis; an experimentation or causal-inference project; and, if relevant, a text or LLM project such as a RAG bot. Each has a short README with the problem, approach, result and how to run. Choose data you find interesting, so you can talk about it with energy. Quality beats quantity. The catch: avoid the most overused datasets like Titanic and Iris unless you add something genuinely new, since interviewers have seen hundreds.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Choose three projects for a product-analyst role","html":"<p>Target: product and analytics roles at consumer apps.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Read the target jobs:</strong> SQL, A/B testing, metrics, dashboards and some modelling.</li>\n<li><strong>Project 1 (SQL and BI):</strong> &quot;Where do users drop out of the checkout funnel?&quot; Public e-commerce event data: cohort retention and funnel queries in SQL, plus a dashboard with three charts and one recommendation.</li>\n<li><strong>Project 2 (experimentation):</strong> analyse a public A/B test dataset (for example a mobile game&#39;s gate placement): sample-size check, a sample ratio mismatch check, the effect with a confidence interval, guardrails and a one-page decision memo.</li>\n<li><strong>Project 3 (modelling):</strong> a churn or conversion model with a time-based split, a baseline, a business metric and error analysis.</li>\n<li><strong>Make each one quick to scan:</strong> a README with the question, the answer in two sentences, one chart and a &quot;how to run&quot; section. A recruiter spends about a minute on each.</li>\n<li><strong>Check the coverage:</strong> SQL depth (1), statistics and product judgement (2), ML rigour (3). No two projects show the same skill.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A portfolio is evidence for a specific job. Three projects that each prove a different required skill beat ten similar notebooks.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-how-to-choose","text":"How to choose","level":3},{"id":"h-scoping-advice","text":"Scoping advice","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Type</th><th>Project ideas (India-relevant)</th><th>Skills shown</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Classification (business)</strong></td><td>Customer churn (telecom/OTT/SaaS); loan default/credit risk with WoE/IV scorecard; UPI/credit-card fraud detection</td><td>Imbalance, feature engineering, metric choice, explainability</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Forecasting / regression</strong></td><td>Demand forecasting for retail/quick commerce (Kaggle M5, Rossmann); delivery-time (ETA) prediction; used-car price (Cars24/CarDekho data)</td><td>Time-aware CV, lag features, error analysis</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>NLP</strong></td><td>Sentiment/aspect analysis of Zomato/Amazon/Play Store reviews; support-ticket classification; resume-JD matching; Hinglish sentiment</td><td>Text preprocessing, TF-IDF baseline vs transformer</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Recommender</strong></td><td>Movie/restaurant/product recommender with popularity, item-item CF, and matrix factorization; evaluate Precision@K/NDCG</td><td>Cold start, ranking metrics</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>GenAI/LLM</strong></td><td>RAG chatbot on RBI/SEBI circulars, insurance policy PDFs or company filings; text-to-SQL assistant; report summarizer</td><td>Chunking, embeddings, vector DB, evaluation</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Analytics / experimentation</strong></td><td>Cohort/retention + funnel analysis in SQL; A/B test analysis on a public dataset (Cookie Cats, Criteo uplift); RFM segmentation; Power BI/Tableau dashboard</td><td>Business storytelling, SQL, stats</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><strong>Deployment / engineering</strong></td><td>Serve any of the above with FastAPI + Docker; Streamlit app; deploy to Render/Hugging Face Spaces/AWS/GCP free tier; add MLflow tracking</td><td>Production readiness</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><strong>Big data (optional)</strong></td><td>PySpark pipeline on large NYC taxi or e-commerce events dataset; Airflow DAG</td><td>Scale and pipelines</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Data sources:</strong> Kaggle, UCI, Google Dataset Search, data.gov.in, RBI/NSO/NSE datasets, Open-Meteo, public APIs, Hugging Face datasets, your own scraped data (respect ToS).</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Avoid:</strong> Titanic/Iris/MNIST as headline projects, tutorial copies without original thinking, notebook dumps with no narrative.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-how-to-choose\">How to choose</h3>\n<p>Aim for <strong>3 to 4 projects from different categories</strong>, at least one with an end-to-end delivery (API or app), and one linked to the type of company you are targeting (credit risk for BFSI, A/B analysis for product companies, RAG for AI-first startups).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Type</th><th>India-relevant ideas</th><th>Skills shown</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Classification (business)</strong></td><td>telecom/OTT churn; loan default with a WoE/IV scorecard; UPI or credit-card fraud</td><td>imbalance, feature engineering, metric choice, explainability</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Forecasting / regression</strong></td><td>retail or quick-commerce demand (Kaggle M5, Rossmann); delivery-time (ETA); used-car price (Cars24/CarDekho-style data)</td><td>time-aware validation, lag features, error analysis</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>NLP</strong></td><td>sentiment and aspects from Zomato/Amazon/Play Store reviews; support-ticket classification; resume-JD matching; Hinglish sentiment</td><td>text preprocessing, TF-IDF baseline vs transformer</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Recommender</strong></td><td>movie/restaurant/product recommender with popularity, item-item CF and matrix factorisation; evaluate Precision@K and NDCG</td><td>cold start, ranking metrics</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>GenAI / LLM</strong></td><td>RAG chatbot over RBI/SEBI circulars, insurance policies or company filings; text-to-SQL assistant; report summariser</td><td>chunking, embeddings, vector DB, evaluation</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Analytics / experimentation</strong></td><td>cohort, retention and funnel analysis in SQL; A/B analysis on a public dataset (Cookie Cats, Criteria uplift data); RFM segmentation; Power BI/Tableau dashboard</td><td>storytelling, SQL, statistics</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><strong>Deployment / engineering</strong></td><td>serve any project with FastAPI + Docker; Streamlit app; deploy to Render/Hugging Face Spaces/AWS/GCP free tier; add MLflow tracking</td><td>production readiness</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><strong>Big data (optional)</strong></td><td>PySpark pipeline on large NYC taxi or e-commerce events data; an Airflow DAG</td><td>scale and pipelines</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Data sources:</strong> Kaggle, UCI, Google Dataset Search, data.gov.in, RBI/NSO/NSE datasets, Open-Meteo, public APIs, Hugging Face datasets, your own scraped data (respect terms of service).</p>\n<p><strong>Avoid:</strong> Titanic/Iris/MNIST as headline projects, tutorial copies without original thinking, notebook dumps with no narrative.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-scoping-advice\">Scoping advice</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Write the <strong>one-sentence decision</strong> the project supports before touching data (&quot;which customers should receive the retention offer?&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Time-box:</strong> two to three weeks per project, following the workflow checklist.</li>\n<li>Prefer a <strong>smaller dataset done well</strong> (proper validation, error analysis, README) over a huge one done carelessly.</li>\n<li>Reuse one project for delivery: take your best model and wrap it with FastAPI and Docker.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m14-portfolio~1","q":"You are targeting BFSI roles. Which three projects do you build?","a":"<p>(1) A <strong>credit-risk scorecard</strong>: logistic regression with WoE binning and IV feature selection, KS, AUC/Gini, PSI for stability, and a clear interpretation of each variable. (2) A <strong>fraud or default prediction</strong> project emphasising class imbalance, cost-based threshold selection and leakage control. (3) A <strong>delivery piece</strong>: a FastAPI scoring service or a Power BI/Tableau dashboard of portfolio risk (vintages, delinquency buckets), showing you can communicate to risk teams. Mention SQL/SAS skills in the README.</p>\n","s":"A credit-risk scorecard with WoE/IV, a fraud or default classification project with imbalance handling, and a deployed scoring API or dashboard."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-portfolio~ca0_1","q":"You can do only three projects. Which mix?","a":"<p>One SQL/analytics case with a dashboard, one end-to-end ML model with validation and error analysis, and one experimentation or LLM/RAG project; this shows range.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-portfolio~ca0_2","q":"How do you make a common dataset stand out?","a":"<p>Ask a new question, enrich it with another source, deploy it, or add rigorous evaluation and business framing.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-portfolio~ca0_3","q":"What should a README contain?","a":"<p>Problem, data, approach, key results, limitations, and how to run it.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m14-portfolio~ta1_1","q":"<p>You are targeting a fintech lender. Propose three projects, one sentence each, saying which skill each one proves.</p>\n","a":"<p>(1) A credit default model with logistic regression and a scorecard (weight of evidence, KS and Gini), with reason codes for each decision: proves interpretable risk modelling. (2) An SQL analysis of repayment cohorts by month of disbursal (vintage curves) with a small dashboard: proves SQL and portfolio monitoring. (3) A fraud or anomaly detection project on transaction data, with a precision-at-capacity metric and a cost-based threshold: proves handling of imbalance and business-cost thinking.</p>\n"}]},"m14-template":{"id":"m14-template","slug":"project-template-and-workflow-checklist","m":14,"title":"Project template and workflow checklist","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m14-portfolio"],"tags":["projects","workflow"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"A repository template for each project, a README structure that recruiters read, and the workflow checklist for modelling projects with the reasoning behind each item.","keyTerms":["project structure","README","reproducibility","baseline","pipeline","threshold","explainability","deployment plan"],"interviewQs":["How do you structure a data science repository?","What is in your README?"],"mistakes":["Committing large data files or secrets","No instructions to run the project","No results table comparing to a baseline"],"practice":"Create a repository skeleton and write a README with the eight sections for a project you have done.","related":["m14-strong-project","m11-dev-tools"],"points":"README structure: 1) Problem & business value, 2) Data (source, size, grain), 3) Approach, 4) Results (table with baseline vs final), 5) Key insights and recommendations, 6) Limitations and next steps, 7) How to run, 8) Demo link/screenshots.\n\n- Define target, prediction time, and what data is available then\n- Split correctly (time-based or group-based where needed) before any fitting\n- Baseline (dummy, simple linear/logistic, seasonal naive)\n- Pipeline for preprocessing (no leakage)\n- Cross-validation and appropriate metrics\n- Hyperparameter tuning and comparison of 2-3 model families\n- Error analysis by segment; look at worst predictions\n- Explainability and sanity checks\n- Threshold selection tied to business cost\n- Final test-set evaluation (once)\n- Deployment + monitoring plan\n- Clear business recommendation with quantified impact estimate"},"intuitive":"<p>A project folder is like a tidy lab: data in one place, exploration notebooks in another, reusable code in a third, and a front door (the README) that tells visitors what this is, how well it worked, and how to run it. A recruiter gives a repository about two minutes; the README decides whether they keep reading. The checklist is a flight checklist: every modelling project should tick the same boxes so you never forget the important ones.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Repository quality signals engineering hygiene, which many candidates lack.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Standard structure, a clear README with a results table, and the twelve-step checklist.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;I use a repeatable workflow for projects. One: define the problem, the user and the metric. Two: collect data and document its source and limits. Three: explore and clean, recording decisions. Four: set a baseline, then engineer features and try a few models with cross-validation. Five: evaluate on a held-out test set, with error analysis and a threshold chosen from costs. Six: interpret, using feature importance or SHAP, and check fairness and robustness. Seven: package it as a script, an API or an app. Eight: write the story. Keeping a checklist stops me from skipping the unglamorous steps. The catch: write down why I made each choice while I make it, since that becomes the interview answer.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"A four-week project plan with a decision log","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Milestone</th><th>Decision log entries</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Question, data, EDA</td><td>why this target; why this prediction date</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Baseline and features</td><td>why this split; which features were dropped for leakage</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Models and error analysis</td><td>why model B over model A; where it fails</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Delivery and write-up</td><td>what you would do with more time</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Week 1:</strong> write the question and the decision in two sentences, get the data, and record its grain, size and quality issues.</li>\n<li><strong>Week 2:</strong> build a simple baseline end to end (data in, metric out) before any tuning. It catches pipeline bugs early.</li>\n<li><strong>Week 3:</strong> try two or three models with the same validation; analyse errors by segment.</li>\n<li><strong>Week 4:</strong> package it (an app, an API or a clean notebook), write the README, and record the limitations.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep the decision log as you go.</strong> One line each: date, decision, reason, alternative rejected. For example: &quot;12 May: split by month, not at random, because the model predicts future months; a random split scored 0.05 higher, a sign of leakage.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>The log becomes your interview answers.</strong> Every deep-dive question (&quot;why this metric?&quot;) has a written answer with a date.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A plan with weekly milestones keeps the project finished and honest, and the decision log turns your work into ready answers.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-14-3","text":"14.3 Project build template (follow for each)","level":2},{"id":"c-14-4","text":"14.4 Workflow checklist for each modeling project","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-structure","text":"The structure","level":3},{"id":"h-the-readme-structure-with-an-example-skeleton","text":"The README structure, with an example skeleton","level":3},{"id":"h-the-workflow-checklist-with-the-why","text":"The workflow checklist, with the why","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-14-3\"><span class=\"sec-num\">14.3</span> Project build template (follow for each)</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">project-name/\n  README.md              &lt;- problem, data, approach, results, how to run, limitations\n  data/                  &lt;- (small samples or download script; not large files)\n  notebooks/             &lt;- 01_eda.ipynb, 02_baseline.ipynb, 03_modeling.ipynb\n  src/                   &lt;- reusable python modules (features.py, train.py, predict.py)\n  app/                   &lt;- FastAPI/Streamlit code\n  tests/                 &lt;- a few unit tests\n  requirements.txt\n  Dockerfile\n  reports/               &lt;- figures, final summary</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>README structure:</strong> 1) Problem &amp; business value, 2) Data (source, size, grain), 3) Approach, 4) Results (table with baseline vs final), 5) Key insights and recommendations, 6) Limitations and next steps, 7) How to run, 8) Demo link/screenshots.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-14-4\"><span class=\"sec-num\">14.4</span> Workflow checklist for each modeling project</h2>"},{"k":"cl","id":"m14-template.c1","kind":"list","items":["Define target, prediction time, and what data is available then","Split correctly (time-based or group-based where needed) <strong>before</strong> any fitting","Baseline (dummy, simple linear/logistic, seasonal naive)","Pipeline for preprocessing (no leakage)","Cross-validation and appropriate metrics","Hyperparameter tuning and comparison of 2-3 model families","Error analysis by segment; look at worst predictions","Explainability and sanity checks","Threshold selection tied to business cost","Final test-set evaluation (once)","Deployment + monitoring plan","Clear business recommendation with quantified impact estimate"]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-structure\">The structure</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">project-name/\n  README.md              &lt;- problem, data, approach, results, how to run, limitations\n  data/                  &lt;- (small samples or a download script; not large files)\n  notebooks/             &lt;- 01_eda.ipynb, 02_baseline.ipynb, 03_modeling.ipynb\n  src/                   &lt;- reusable python modules (features.py, train.py, predict.py)\n  app/                   &lt;- FastAPI/Streamlit code\n  tests/                 &lt;- a few unit tests\n  requirements.txt\n  Dockerfile\n  reports/               &lt;- figures, final summary</code></pre></div>\n<p>Keep notebooks for exploration and move stable code into <code>src/</code> so training is reproducible with one command (<code>python -m src.train</code>). Add a <code>.gitignore</code> for data, <code>.env</code> and model binaries.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-readme-structure-with-an-example-skeleton\">The README structure, with an example skeleton</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Problem and business value</strong>: &quot;Predict which telecom customers will churn within 30 days so the retention team can target offers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Data</strong>: source, size, time span, grain, licence.</li>\n<li><strong>Approach</strong>: split strategy, features, models, validation.</li>\n<li><strong>Results</strong>: a table with baseline vs final.</li>\n<li><strong>Key insights and recommendations</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Limitations and next steps</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>How to run</strong>: commands.</li>\n<li><strong>Demo link / screenshots</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Example results table:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>PR-AUC</th><th>Recall @ 30% contacted</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Logistic regression baseline</td><td>0.46</td><td>0.52</td><td>scaled features, class weights</td></tr><tr><td>LightGBM (final)</td><td>0.63</td><td>0.71</td><td>tuned with time-based CV</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-workflow-checklist-with-the-why\">The workflow checklist, with the why</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Define target, prediction time and available data</strong> (prevents leakage and scope confusion)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Split correctly before any fitting</strong> (time or group-based where needed)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Baseline</strong> (dummy, simple linear/logistic, seasonal naive) so you know what &quot;good&quot; means</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Pipeline for preprocessing</strong> (no leakage)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Cross-validation and appropriate metrics</strong></li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Hyperparameter tuning and comparison of two or three model families</strong></li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Error analysis by segment; look at the worst predictions</strong> (finds data bugs and unfairness)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Explainability and sanity checks</strong> (do the drivers make sense?)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Threshold selection tied to business cost</strong></li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Final test-set evaluation (once)</strong></li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Deployment and monitoring plan</strong></li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>A clear business recommendation with a quantified impact estimate</strong></li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m14-template~1","q":"What goes in the &quot;Limitations&quot; section of a README, and why include it?","a":"<p>Data limits (size, time span, label noise, geography), modelling assumptions (for example stationary behaviour), known failure modes found in error analysis, what was <em>not</em> tested (other segments, longer horizons), and what would be needed for production (monitoring, retraining, fairness checks). Including it shows maturity and honesty, and it pre-empts the interviewer&#39;s hardest questions.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-template~ca0_1","q":"Why keep a decision log during a project?","a":"<p>It records why each choice was made, giving ready, honest answers in a deep-dive interview.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-template~ca0_2","q":"Where does the test set first get used?","a":"<p>Once, after all modelling choices are final, to report an unbiased estimate.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-template~ca0_3","q":"Which step do beginners most often skip?","a":"<p>Baselines and error analysis; they jump to complex models and report a single score.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m14-template~ta1_1","q":"<p>Write three decision-log lines for a house-price project: the target, the metric and the validation split.</p>\n","a":"<p>Examples: &quot;Target: log of sale price, because prices are right-skewed and errors should be relative; the alternative, raw price, was dominated by a few mansions.&quot; &quot;Metric: RMSE on the log price (about a percentage error), also reported as the median absolute error in rupees for stakeholders.&quot; &quot;Split: train on sales before 2024, test on 2024, because the model will price future listings; a random split leaked neighbourhood trends from the future.&quot;</p>\n"}]},"m14-resume-bullets":{"id":"m14-resume-bullets","slug":"turning-a-project-into-resume-bullets","m":14,"title":"Turning a project into resume bullets","min":15,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m14-strong-project"],"tags":["projects","resume"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"How to turn a project into resume bullets using the action-technique-result formula, with before-and-after rewrites.","keyTerms":["action verb","technique","quantified result","context","reproducible numbers"],"interviewQs":["Walk me through this number on your resume.","How did you measure that impact?"],"mistakes":["Listing tools with no outcomes","Fabricating or rounding up numbers","Vague verbs like \"worked on\""],"practice":"Rewrite three of your current resume bullets with the formula and confirm every number is reproducible from your repo.","related":["m15-resume","m14-deep-dive"],"points":"Formula: Action verb + what you built + technique/tools + quantified result + context.\n\n- \"Built a churn prediction model (LightGBM, SMOTE-free class weighting) on 7k telecom customers; improved PR-AUC from 0.46 (logistic baseline) to 0.63; top-decile lift of 3.1x, enabling targeted retention.\"\n- \"Designed a RAG assistant over 200 pages of insurance policy PDFs (FAISS + sentence-transformers + LLM API); reached 86% faithfulness on a 60-question golden set; deployed via FastAPI + Docker.\"\n- \"Analyzed 1.2M e-commerce events in SQL/pandas; identified 38% drop-off at payment step on Android, suggesting a fix projected to add ~4% conversion.\"\n\nDo not fabricate numbers. Every figure must be reproducible from your repo."},"intuitive":"<p>A resume bullet is a tiny advertisement for one thing you did. It works best when it says what you built, how, and what it achieved, in one line, with a number you can defend. &quot;Used machine learning for churn&quot; says nothing; &quot;Built a churn model that found 3x more at-risk customers in the top 10% than random, enabling targeted offers&quot; tells a story. And every number must survive the question &quot;how did you get that?&quot;.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers pick bullets to interrogate; good bullets steer the conversation to your strengths.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Verb + what + technique/tools + quantified result + context. No fabricated numbers.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A good resume bullet has an action verb, what I did, how, and the measurable result. A formula: accomplished X, measured by Y, by doing Z. Instead of &#39;built a churn model&#39;, I write &#39;built a gradient boosting churn model on 200k customers, lifting recall at top decile from 18 to 31 per cent, projected to save about 120k dollars a year in retention offers&#39;. Numbers show impact; tools show skills, so I name them once. I keep each bullet to one or two lines and put the strongest first. If I do not have business numbers, I use scale, speed or accuracy. The catch: never claim a number I cannot defend, since I will be asked how I measured it.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Rewrite a bullet in four passes","html":"<p><strong>Start:</strong> &quot;Worked on a machine learning model for churn.&quot;</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Use a strong verb and say what you built:</strong> &quot;Built a churn prediction model.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Add the method and the scale:</strong> &quot;Built a gradient-boosted churn model on 200,000 telecom customers using 30 behavioural features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Add the result against a baseline:</strong> &quot;...raising recall in the top 10% riskiest customers from 18% (rule-based) to 31%.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Add the business meaning, honestly estimated:</strong> &quot;...enough to target about 1,300 more at-risk customers a month at the same calling cost.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Final:</strong> &quot;Built a gradient-boosted churn model on 200,000 telecom customers, raising top-decile recall from 18% to 31% over the rule-based baseline, enough to reach about 1,300 more at-risk customers a month at the same cost.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Check that you can defend every number:</strong> where it came from, how it was measured and on which data. If you cannot, remove it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Verb, what, how, scale, result against a baseline, impact. Each number invites a good interview question.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-formula-and-examples","text":"The formula and examples","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Formula:</strong> Action verb + what you built + technique/tools + quantified result + context.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>&quot;Built a churn prediction model (LightGBM, SMOTE-free class weighting) on 7k telecom customers; improved PR-AUC from 0.46 (logistic baseline) to 0.63; top-decile lift of 3.1x, enabling targeted retention.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Designed a RAG assistant over 200 pages of insurance policy PDFs (FAISS + sentence-transformers + LLM API); reached 86% faithfulness on a 60-question golden set; deployed via FastAPI + Docker.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Analyzed 1.2M e-commerce events in SQL/pandas; identified 38% drop-off at payment step on Android, suggesting a fix projected to add ~4% conversion.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Do not fabricate numbers. Every figure must be reproducible from your repo.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-formula-and-examples\">The formula and examples</h3>\n<p><strong>Formula:</strong> Action verb + what you built + technique/tools + quantified result + context.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Weak</th><th>Strong</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Worked on churn prediction using machine learning.&quot;</td><td>&quot;Built a churn prediction model (LightGBM, class weighting) on 7k telecom customers; improved PR-AUC from 0.46 (logistic baseline) to 0.63 and top-decile lift to 3.1x, enabling targeted retention.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Did analysis of e-commerce data.&quot;</td><td>&quot;Analysed 1.2M e-commerce events in SQL/pandas; found a 38% drop-off at the payment step on Android, suggesting a fix projected to add about 4% conversion.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Made a chatbot.&quot;</td><td>&quot;Designed a RAG assistant over 200 pages of insurance policy PDFs (FAISS + sentence-transformers + LLM API); reached 86% faithfulness on a 60-question golden set; deployed with FastAPI and Docker.&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Rules:</strong> strong verbs (built, designed, reduced, improved, automated); compare to a <strong>baseline</strong>; include <strong>scale</strong> (rows, users, documents); give the <strong>business effect</strong> or a clearly labelled projection; keep to 1 to 2 lines.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout trap\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do not fabricate numbers</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>Every figure must be <strong>reproducible from your repo</strong>. If you cannot regenerate &quot;86% faithfulness&quot; from your evaluation script, remove it. Labelled estimates (&quot;projected&quot;) are fine when they are honest.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m14-resume-bullets~1","q":"An interviewer points at &quot;improved PR-AUC from 0.46 to 0.63&quot; and asks &quot;how did you validate that?&quot;. What should you be ready to explain?","a":"<p>The split (time-based or stratified CV), the size and class balance of the evaluation set, how the baseline was tuned (fairly), whether the improvement is statistically meaningful (confidence intervals or multiple folds), the absence of leakage, and what 0.63 means operationally (for example recall at the contact budget). Have the evaluation notebook ready to show.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-resume-bullets~ca0_1","q":"Rewrite: &quot;Worked on a recommendation system.&quot;","a":"<p>&quot;Built a collaborative-filtering recommender on 1M interactions in PySpark, improving offline NDCG@10 from 0.21 to 0.29 over a popularity baseline&quot; (use numbers you can defend).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-resume-bullets~ca0_2","q":"Course project with no business number. What metric can you quote?","a":"<p>Model performance versus a baseline, data size, runtime reduction, or time saved by automation.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-resume-bullets~ca0_3","q":"Why avoid inflated numbers?","a":"<p>Interviewers probe them; if you cannot explain how it was measured, credibility drops across the whole resume.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m14-resume-bullets~ta1_1","q":"<p>Rewrite this bullet: &quot;Made dashboards in Power BI for the sales team.&quot;</p>\n","a":"<p>For example: &quot;Built a Power BI sales dashboard (pipeline, conversion by region, weekly targets) used by 25 sales managers, replacing a weekly manual Excel report and saving about 6 analyst hours a week.&quot; Use only numbers you can confirm, such as the user count and the hours saved; if you cannot confirm them, describe the decision the dashboard supports instead.</p>\n"}]},"m14-deep-dive":{"id":"m14-deep-dive","slug":"the-project-deep-dive-questions","m":14,"title":"The project deep-dive questions","min":40,"type":"practice","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m14-strong-project"],"tags":["projects","interview-questions"],"qa":20,"ai":{"scope":"The sixteen project deep-dive questions, with what a strong answer includes and a short example for each, plus advice for experienced candidates.","keyTerms":["problem and data","preparation","modelling","evaluation and impact","production thinking","ownership"],"interviewQs":["Why did you choose this algorithm and what else did you try?","How did you avoid leakage?","What would you do differently with more time?"],"mistakes":["Answering with generalities instead of specifics from your project","Claiming team work as your own","Not knowing the numbers in your own README"],"practice":"Answer all sixteen questions for your best project in writing, then record yourself answering five aloud.","related":["m14-strong-project","m14-resume-bullets","m16-self-test"],"points":"Problem and data\n\nQ: Why this problem? Who would use the output and what decision changes?\nA: \n\nQ: Where did the data come from? Size, time span, grain, quality issues?\nA: \n\nQ: How did you define the target? Any ambiguity or label noise?\nA: \n\nPreparation\n\nQ: How did you handle missing values, outliers, duplicates, and categorical variables, and why?\nA: \n\nQ: Which features mattered? Which did you engineer and what was the logic?\nA: \n\nQ: How did you avoid leakage?\nA: \n\nModeling\n\nQ: Why this algorithm? What else did you try and why did it lose?\nA: \n\nQ: How did you split and validate? Why that strategy?\nA: \n\nQ: Which metric did you optimize and why? Why not accuracy?\nA: \n\nQ: How did you tune hyperparameters? What was the final configuration?\nA: \n\nQ: How did you handle class imbalance?\nA: \n\nEvaluation and impact\n\nQ: What are the model's failure modes? Where does it perform worst?\nA: \n\nQ: How do you explain predictions to stakeholders?\nA: \n\nQ: What is the estimated business impact or cost/benefit?\nA: \n\nProduction thinking\n\nQ: How would you deploy, monitor, and retrain it? What could go wrong (drift, latency, feature availability)?\nA: \n\nQ: What would you do differently with more time/data?\nA: \n\nWork-experience candidates (1-2 years): prepare a story with situation, your specific contribution, stakeholder dynamics, a technical obstacle, how you measured impact, and a lesson learned. Interviewers check ownership: \"What did you do versus the team?\""},"intuitive":"<p>These sixteen questions are what a curious interviewer will ask about any project, in roughly this order: why, where did the data come from, how did you prepare it, how did you test it, how well did it work, and how would it survive in the real world. The best preparation is to write out your real answers in advance, with numbers, then practise saying them out loud in two minutes or less.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers often spend thirty to fifty per cent of a technical round on your projects.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Prepare specific answers with real numbers for all sixteen.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;In a project deep dive, the interviewer probes each decision. I prepare a two-minute story: the problem, the data, my approach, the result, and what I learned. Then I expect questions on why this metric, how I validated, how I handled missing data and leakage, why this model over simpler ones, what the errors looked like, how I would deploy and monitor it, and what I would do differently. I answer with the choice, the reason, and the trade-off. If I did not do something, I say so and describe how I would. The catch: honest ownership beats bluffing; I distinguish what I did from what the team did.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Answer \"Why did you use that metric?\" in three parts","html":"<p>Project: a fraud model. The interviewer asks why you used PR-AUC and not accuracy.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The choice:</strong> &quot;I optimised PR-AUC during model selection and reported precision at the review team&#39;s capacity of 200 cases a day.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>The reason, with a number:</strong> &quot;Fraud was 0.4% of transactions, so a model that never flags anything is 99.6% accurate. Accuracy could not tell good models from useless ones. PR-AUC focuses on how well the model ranks the rare positive class.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>The trade-off you accepted:</strong> &quot;PR-AUC is harder to explain to the business, so for them I translated it into precision at 200: 38% of reviewed cases were fraud, against 9% under the old rules.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>The follow-up you invite:</strong> &quot;If they could review more cases, I would move the threshold and report the cost per extra fraud caught.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Keep it short:</strong> about 30 seconds, then stop and let the interviewer choose where to go next.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Choice, reason with a number, trade-off. The same three parts answer &quot;why this split?&quot;, &quot;why this model?&quot; and &quot;why these features?&quot;.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-how-to-use-the-answers-below","text":"How to use the answers below","level":3},{"id":"h-for-candidates-with-work-experience-1-to-2-years","text":"For candidates with work experience (1 to 2 years)","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Problem and data</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#1","src":"c","q":"Why this problem? Who would use the output and what decision changes?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> name the user and the decision, and the cost of the current approach. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Retention managers were giving blanket discounts to everyone who lapsed, costing about 18% of margin. The model ranks customers by churn risk so they can target only the top decile, which is the decision it changes.&quot;</p>\n","s":"State the decision the output changes and who acts on it, with the cost of a wrong prediction."},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#2","src":"c","q":"Where did the data come from? Size, time span, grain, quality issues?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> source, size, time span, grain, known quality issues and licence. <em>Example:</em> &quot;A public telecom dataset with 7,043 customers, one row per customer at a single snapshot; 26.5% churn; some <code>TotalCharges</code> were blank strings for new customers which I converted and imputed with zero tenure logic.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Give the source, size, time span, grain (one row per what), and the main quality issues and how you handled them."},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#3","src":"c","q":"How did you define the target? Any ambiguity or label noise?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> the exact definition, why that window, and acknowledged ambiguity or noise. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Churn = cancelled within the observation period as recorded in the source. I noted that the dataset gives no time dimension, so I could not define a prediction horizon; in production I would define churn as no order in the next 30 days from a snapshot date.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Define the target precisely, the prediction window, and how ambiguity or label noise was handled or measured."},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Preparation</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#4","src":"c","q":"How did you handle missing values, outliers, duplicates, and categorical variables, and why?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> each choice with the reason. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Missing <code>TotalCharges</code> (11 rows) were new customers, so zero was right; I used median imputation inside a pipeline for other numerics, one-hot for low-cardinality categoricals, and checked duplicates. I capped monthly charges at the 99th percentile after confirming they were genuine but extreme.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Describe each fix and its reason: investigate missingness, flag it, impute with training statistics; cap or keep outliers deliberately; drop duplicates; encode categories by cardinality."},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#5","src":"c","q":"Which features mattered? Which did you engineer and what was the logic?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> top features with business logic and any engineered ones. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Contract type, tenure and monthly charges dominated. I engineered <code>charges_per_tenure_month</code> and a flag for customers with multiple add-ons but no tech support; SHAP confirmed month-to-month contracts and short tenure as the strongest drivers, which matches domain intuition.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Name the top features, how you found them (importance or SHAP), and the logic behind two engineered ones."},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#6","src":"c","q":"How did you avoid leakage?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> a concrete leakage audit. <em>Example:</em> &quot;I fit all transformers inside a scikit-learn Pipeline so cross-validation refit them per fold, removed a column recording the cancellation reason since it only exists after churn, and checked that no feature was derived from the target. A suspiciously perfect feature would have been a red flag; none appeared.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Split first, fit preprocessing inside a pipeline, split by time or group, and check each feature will exist at prediction time.","m":"<p><strong>Checklist to quote.</strong> Features available at prediction time; preprocessing fit on training folds only; no duplicate or same-entity rows across split; time-ordered split for temporal data; suspicion of near-perfect scores.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Modeling</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#7","src":"c","q":"Why this algorithm? What else did you try and why did it lose?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> compare at least two families with reasons. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Logistic regression was the interpretable baseline; LightGBM beat it on PR-AUC (0.63 vs 0.46) by capturing interactions between contract type and tenure. A random forest was close but slower to tune; a neural net was overkill for 7k rows. I kept the logistic model as a documented fallback.&quot;</p>\n","s":"A baseline first, then the final model with the measured gain; the losing models and the reasons (interpretability, speed, accuracy)."},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#8","src":"c","q":"How did you split and validate? Why that strategy?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> the split and why it fits the problem. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Stratified 5-fold CV for model selection and a held-out 20% test set used once. With real data I would split by time (train on earlier months, test on later) because customers&#39; behaviour drifts.&quot;</p>\n","s":"The split matches production use (time-based or group-based), with cross-validation for tuning and an untouched test set."},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#9","src":"c","q":"Which metric did you optimize and why? Why not accuracy?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> a metric tied to cost, and why not accuracy. <em>Example:</em> &quot;PR-AUC for ranking, recall within the 30% of customers the retention team can contact, and expected profit at the chosen threshold. Accuracy would be 73% by always predicting &#39;no churn&#39;.&quot;</p>\n","s":"The metric tied to the cost of errors (for example recall at fixed precision or PR-AUC), and why accuracy misleads here.","m":"<p><strong>Example.</strong> For fraud at 0.5% prevalence, say: &quot;accuracy would be 99.5% for a model that finds nothing; I optimised recall at a precision of at least 40%, because reviewers can handle limited alerts.&quot;</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#10","src":"c","q":"How did you tune hyperparameters? What was the final configuration?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> the method, the search space, and the final values with discipline about the test set. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Randomised search, 40 trials, on <code>num_leaves</code> (8 to 64), <code>learning_rate</code> (0.01 to 0.2, log scale), <code>min_child_samples</code> and subsampling, using 5-fold stratified CV on the training data with early stopping; final: 31 leaves, 0.05 learning rate, about 350 trees. The test set was touched once.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Random or Bayesian search with cross-validation on the training data; give the final settings and the sensitivity."},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#11","src":"c","q":"How did you handle class imbalance?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> what you tried and what worked. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Churn was 26.5% (mild imbalance), so I used stratified splits and <code>class_weight</code> first, then tuned the threshold on validation for F1 and cost rather than using 0.5. SMOTE gave no gain in PR-AUC, so I dropped it.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Stratified splits, class weights or resampling in training folds only, a tuned threshold, and PR-based metrics.","m":"<p><strong>Order of tries.</strong> Class weights and threshold tuning first (no data changes), then undersampling or SMOTE inside the training folds, and PR-AUC as the metric throughout.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Evaluation and impact</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#12","src":"c","q":"What are the model&#39;s failure modes? Where does it perform worst?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> specific segments and examples where it fails. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Error analysis showed lower recall for customers with tenure under three months (very little history) and for fibre-optic customers on month-to-month contracts, where churn is driven by service issues not in the data. I documented these as limitations.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Slice errors by segment and feature ranges, and describe where it fails (rare classes, new users) and what you tried.","m":"<p><strong>How to find failure modes.</strong> Group predictions by segment (region, device, new versus old users) and by feature bins; compare error metrics; read 20 mistakes by hand to find patterns.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#13","src":"c","q":"How do you explain predictions to stakeholders?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> global and local explanations in plain words. <em>Example:</em> &quot;SHAP summary plots for global drivers and per-customer waterfall plots for the retention agent: &#39;high risk because month-to-month contract, 4 months tenure, and two support tickets&#39;. I avoid causal language since drivers are associations.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Global drivers with importance, local explanations with SHAP, in business language, plus example cases."},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#14","src":"c","q":"What is the estimated business impact or cost/benefit?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> a transparent, assumption-labelled estimate. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Per 1,000 customers with 26.5% churn there are about 265 churners. Contacting the top 30% (300 customers) captures 71% of them, about 188 churners (63% precision). With an offer costing ₹300, a 30% save rate and a ₹6,000 customer value, that is 56 saved customers × ₹6,000 = ₹3.4 lakh of retained value against ₹0.9 lakh of offer cost, a net of roughly ₹2.5 lakh per 1,000 customers. It is a projection that depends on the save-rate assumption, which an A/B test should confirm.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Estimate benefit minus cost with the confusion matrix and costs per error type; state assumptions and uncertainty."},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Production thinking</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#15","src":"c","q":"How would you deploy, monitor, and retrain it? What could go wrong (drift, latency, feature availability)?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> batch vs real-time choice, serving, monitoring and retraining. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Weekly batch scoring in a scheduled job, model packaged in a Docker image with a FastAPI endpoint for ad-hoc scoring. Monitor PSI on inputs and score distribution, track offer conversion, and retrain monthly or when PSI exceeds 0.25. Risks: drift, feature availability and feedback loops from offers.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Batch or API serving, monitor input drift and performance on delayed labels, scheduled retraining, rollback; risks: drift, latency, training-serving skew.","m":"<p><strong>Say all four.</strong> Serving mode (batch or online); monitoring (data quality, drift, performance with delayed labels); retraining trigger (schedule or drift alert); rollback and canary release.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"14.6#16","src":"c","q":"What would you do differently with more time/data?","a":"<p><strong>Strong answer:</strong> realistic, prioritised improvements. <em>Example:</em> &quot;Get time-stamped data for a proper time-based split; add service-quality features (delivery delays, complaints); try an uplift model to target customers who will respond to an offer, not just those who might churn; and run a live A/B test of the retention policy.&quot;</p>\n","s":"Name a real limitation and a specific improvement: more or fresher data, time-based validation, better features, deployment and monitoring."},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Work-experience candidates (1-2 years):</strong> prepare a story with situation, your specific contribution, stakeholder dynamics, a technical obstacle, how you measured impact, and a lesson learned. Interviewers check ownership: &quot;What did <em>you</em> do versus the team?&quot;</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-how-to-use-the-answers-below\">How to use the answers below</h3>\n<p>For each question the model answer describes <strong>what a strong answer includes</strong> and gives an example from a hypothetical churn project. Replace the details with your own facts.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-for-candidates-with-work-experience-1-to-2-years\">For candidates with work experience (1 to 2 years)</h3>\n<p>Prepare a story with <strong>situation, your specific contribution, stakeholder dynamics, a technical obstacle, how you measured impact, and a lesson learned</strong>. Interviewers check <strong>ownership</strong>: &quot;What did <em>you</em> do versus the team?&quot; Use &quot;I&quot; for your actions, &quot;we&quot; for team outcomes, and be ready to draw the architecture and explain a decision you argued for.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<details class=\"more\"><summary>Prepare six deep-dive answers</summary><div class=\"more-b\"><p>For each of your main projects, write two or three sentences for each of these. <strong>Problem and metric:</strong> why this target and metric, and what a wrong prediction costs. <strong>Data:</strong> size, source, leakage risks, how missing values were handled. <strong>Validation:</strong> the split scheme and why it matches how the model is used. <strong>Model choice:</strong> baseline first, then why the final model beat it by how much. <strong>Errors:</strong> where the model fails and what you tried. <strong>Next steps:</strong> deployment, monitoring, and the first thing you would change with more time. Practise saying them aloud in under two minutes.</p>\n</div></details>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-deep-dive~ca0_1","q":"&quot;Why did you choose XGBoost?&quot; Give a strong answer shape.","a":"<p>Baseline logistic regression scored X; boosting captured non-linear interactions and improved Y; I tuned with CV, and accepted lower interpretability, mitigated with SHAP.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-deep-dive~ca0_2","q":"How do you answer &quot;what would you do differently?&quot;","a":"<p>Name a real limitation and a specific improvement, for example collecting more recent data, better validation by time, or deploying with monitoring.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-deep-dive~ca0_3","q":"You did only part of a team project. How do you describe it?","a":"<p>State clearly what you owned and decided, and describe the rest as team context, without claiming credit.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m14-deep-dive~ta2_1","q":"<p>Answer &quot;How did you validate your model?&quot; in the choice, reason and trade-off format, for a demand-forecasting project.</p>\n","a":"<p>Choice: &quot;I used rolling-origin validation: train on all data up to a month, forecast the next month, and repeat over the last six months.&quot; Reason: &quot;The model is used to forecast the future, so every test had to be in the future relative to training; a random split would leak information about the same weeks.&quot; Trade-off: &quot;Six folds of one month each give a noisier estimate than random folds would, so I reported the mean and the range of MAE across the six months, and kept the final quarter as an untouched test set.&quot;</p>\n"}]},"m14-red-flags":{"id":"m14-red-flags","slug":"red-flags-to-avoid","m":14,"title":"Red flags to avoid","min":10,"type":"revise","priority":"Highest","prereq":[],"tags":["projects","interview-questions"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The project red flags interviewers notice, why each one hurts, and how to fix or avoid it.","keyTerms":["leakage","tuning on test","random split for time","no baseline","fake deployment","copy-paste code"],"interviewQs":["What are the most common mistakes in data science projects?"],"mistakes":["Overclaiming deployment","Not being able to explain code in your own repository"],"practice":"Audit one of your projects against the seven red flags and fix every one you find.","related":["m14-strong-project","m7-leakage"],"points":"- Cannot explain the math or assumptions behind the model in your own project\n- 99% accuracy without questioning leakage or imbalance\n- Tuning on the test set\n- Random split for time-dependent data\n- No baseline\n- Claiming production deployment when it was a notebook\n- Copy-pasted GitHub code you cannot explain line by line"},"intuitive":"<p>Interviewers have seen the same project mistakes hundreds of times, so they recognise them fast. The seven red flags are all about honesty and understanding: results that are too good to be true, shortcuts in how the model was tested, and claims that do not match reality. Fixing them usually takes an afternoon and raises your credibility a lot.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> A single red flag can overshadow a good project.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Question your own results, test properly, and only claim what you did.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Interviewers get worried by a few patterns. Not being able to explain your own project&#39;s choices. Claiming results without a baseline or proper validation. Leakage you did not notice, such as near-perfect accuracy. Using a model for its own sake where a simple method would do. Ignoring the business context and costs. Listing tools you cannot discuss. Blaming others, or bluffing when you do not know. Copy-paste tutorial projects with no original thought. The fix for each is preparation and honesty: know your projects deeply, show a baseline, state limitations, and say &#39;I am not sure, but here is how I would reason&#39;. The catch: a suspiciously perfect score invites a leakage question, so I check first and mention the checks.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Spot the red flags in a project pitch","html":"<p>A candidate says: <em>&quot;I built a fraud model with 99.7% accuracy using deep learning. We used all the columns from the transactions table. The team used my model, and I improved revenue by 30%.&quot;</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>&quot;99.7% accuracy&quot;</strong> on fraud, where positives are well under 1%: probably meaningless, since predicting &quot;no fraud&quot; every time is about as accurate. The interviewer will ask for recall and precision.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Deep learning&quot;</strong> with no baseline: why not logistic regression or gradient boosting? Without a comparison, the choice looks like fashion.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;All the columns&quot;</strong>: did that include chargeback or dispute fields recorded <em>after</em> the fraud? A strong smell of leakage.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;We&quot; and &quot;I&quot; mixed</strong>, and a claimed 30% revenue gain with no way to measure it: credibility drops.</li>\n<li><strong>A better version:</strong> &quot;Fraud was 0.5% of transactions. A logistic baseline reached 0.31 PR-AUC; gradient boosting reached 0.46. I removed three post-event fields that leaked the outcome. At the review team&#39;s capacity, 40% of flagged cases were fraud, against 12% under the old rules. Rolling out the model was a team effort; I built the features and the evaluation.&quot;</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Every red flag has the same cure: real metrics, a baseline, a leakage check, and honest ownership.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-seven-flags-and-the-fix","text":"The seven flags and the fix","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Cannot explain the math or assumptions behind the model in your own project</li>\n<li>99% accuracy without questioning leakage or imbalance</li>\n<li>Tuning on the test set</li>\n<li>Random split for time-dependent data</li>\n<li>No baseline</li>\n<li>Claiming production deployment when it was a notebook</li>\n<li>Copy-pasted GitHub code you cannot explain line by line</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-seven-flags-and-the-fix\">The seven flags and the fix</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Red flag</th><th>Why it hurts</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cannot explain the math or assumptions behind the model</td><td>shows copy-paste learning</td><td>write a half-page explanation of the model in the README and rehearse it</td></tr><tr><td>99% accuracy, unquestioned</td><td>usually leakage or imbalance</td><td>check the baseline rate, use PR-AUC, audit features</td></tr><tr><td>Tuning on the test set</td><td>inflated, dishonest result</td><td>keep the test set untouched until the end</td></tr><tr><td>Random split for time-dependent data</td><td>future leaks into the past</td><td>time-based split</td></tr><tr><td>No baseline</td><td>no way to judge the model</td><td>add dummy and simple baselines</td></tr><tr><td>Claiming production deployment when it was a notebook</td><td>caught immediately by a follow-up</td><td>say &quot;prototype API&quot; and show what you actually built</td></tr><tr><td>Copy-pasted GitHub code you cannot explain line by line</td><td>collapses under probing</td><td>rewrite the core parts yourself and understand each line</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m14-red-flags~1","q":"You realise during an interview that your project has a leakage bug you had not noticed. What do you do?","a":"<p>Say so, calmly: explain how you spotted it, what it means for the reported numbers, and how you would fix and re-evaluate (for example re-run with the feature removed or the split corrected). Honest self-correction under pressure is a strong signal; hiding or defending a flawed result is a red flag.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-red-flags~ca0_1","q":"Your model has 99.5% accuracy on a hard problem. What will an interviewer suspect?","a":"<p>Leakage or a trivial baseline on imbalanced data; be ready to describe the checks you ran.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-red-flags~ca0_2","q":"What is the best response to a question you cannot answer?","a":"<p>Admit it briefly, reason from fundamentals aloud, and say how you would verify.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m14-red-flags~ca0_3","q":"Why is listing 30 tools on a resume risky?","a":"<p>Each is fair game for questions; shallow knowledge shows. List what you can discuss in depth.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m14-red-flags~ta1_1","q":"<p>A resume line says &quot;Expert in Python, R, SQL, Spark, Hadoop, TensorFlow, PyTorch, Kubernetes, Tableau and Power BI.&quot; What is the risk, and how would you fix it?</p>\n","a":"<p>Every tool listed is fair game for questions, and a shallow answer on any of them undermines the claim of expertise everywhere. Keep only the tools you can discuss for five minutes with an example, group them (for example Languages: Python, SQL; ML: scikit-learn, XGBoost; BI: Power BI), and drop the word &quot;expert&quot;. Show depth through project bullets that use the tools instead.</p>\n"}]}});